{"cluster_id": "C-2532", "topic": "The moveDirectory CAS path published a freshly written part", "target": "architecture/manifests-and-refs", "member_ids": ["B051-054", "B051-105"], "canonical_claim": "The `moveDirectory` CAS path published a freshly written part to its final ref outside the `data_parts` lock; after the refactoring, `moveDirectory` becomes a pure staging re-key and `rename_published_refs` is removed. After eager re-keying and precommit-owned publication, `moveDirectory` no longer publishes and the CAS-internal `rename_published_refs` machinery is removed.", "issue_ids": ["B151"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#de-patching-order", "docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#disk-object-storage-transaction-cpp"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2533", "topic": "Content-addressed object storage must use a real deferred transaction", "target": "architecture/manifests-and-refs", "member_ids": ["B051-068"], "canonical_claim": "Content-addressed object storage must use a real deferred transaction rather than `use_fake_transaction`, because per-file autocommit has no commit point for manifest and ref publication.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#register-disk-object-storage-cpp"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2534", "topic": "repointRef's routine-repoint log level was downgraded from WARNING to", "target": "architecture/manifests-and-refs", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-100"], "canonical_claim": "`repointRef`'s routine-repoint log level was downgraded from `WARNING` to `LOG_DEBUG` because, post-all-tree, a standalone write/remove on an already-committed part is the designed mechanism (not an anomaly); the `!resolved` branch, covering a BACKLOG-tracked repointRef non-resolving-key audit gap and currently unreachable, stays at `WARNING`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-15-all-tree-part-files-t12-validation-gate.md#L32-L36"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2535", "topic": "D10: ref-key validation exists on both the encode side", "target": "architecture/manifests-and-refs", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-238"], "canonical_claim": "D10: ref-key validation exists on both the encode side and decode side (`CasRefLogCodec`/`CasRefSnapshotCodec` plus `applyRefLogTxn` preconditions) as intentional defense-in-depth, not duplication to remove \u2014 decode-side validation is what pins forged-input rejection and tests rely on it.", "issue_ids": ["D10"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L531"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2536", "topic": "The controller's Task 5 removal design gives the smallest", "target": "architecture/manifests-and-refs", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-273"], "canonical_claim": "The controller's Task 5 removal design gives the smallest catalog state set as `Creating`, `Live`, `Removing`, `Retired` (four persisted states), where `Absent` is a lookup result rather than a persisted state, and `Decommissioning` is deliberately excluded as a namespace state \u2014 it is a server-root administrative mode that drives affected namespaces through the same four-state transitions rather than a fifth catalog state.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-codex-round2.md#1-with-incarnations-gone-how-namespace-creation-and-removal-would-work"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2537", "topic": "CAS should forbid the Ordinary database engine outright \u2014", "target": "architecture/manifests-and-refs", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-277"], "canonical_claim": "CAS should forbid the `Ordinary` database engine outright \u2014 its UUID-less `data/<db>/<tbl>` layout keeps a table's RootNamespace stable across drop-and-recreate, defeating the incarnation model \u2014 but this alone is insufficient: `ATTACH TABLE ... UUID '<same>'`, explicit-UUID `CREATE TABLE`, replicated-database DDL requiring UUID reuse, and shadow/backup namespace reuse (`FREEZE ... WITH NAME`) remain reachable exact-namespace-reuse routes that a forbid-Ordinary-only policy does not close.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-codex-round2.md#2-is-forbidding-ordinary-with-cas-sufficient", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-fable-round2.md#3-ordinary-databases-forbid-them-anyway-but-it-is-not-the-answer"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2538", "topic": "STAGE B VERDICT (round 2, both reviewers converged): keep", "target": "architecture/manifests-and-refs", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-278"], "canonical_claim": "STAGE B VERDICT (round 2, both reviewers converged): keep the namespace incarnation (option 2) rather than switching to permanent RootNamespace non-reuse or an outside-catalog retirement marker. Permanent non-reuse fails because shadow namespaces are reusable by construction and explicit-UUID attach/replicated-recovery reuse are supported workflows; a permanent `Retired` catalog state is the right shape only if retirement is wanted at all, but costs unbounded O(historical-namespaces) growth on the catalog, which is already a measured write hot spot (137/250 timeout lines).", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-codex-round2.md#3-restated-recommendation", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-fable-round2.md#4-restated-recommendation"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2539", "topic": "With the incarnation kept, no namespace retirement bookkeeping is", "target": "architecture/manifests-and-refs", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-279"], "canonical_claim": "With the incarnation kept, no namespace retirement bookkeeping is needed at all: removal ends in catalog entry deletion, debris becomes inert by foreign incarnation prefix, and the catalog stays O(active namespaces). The existing `_cleanup` marker that gates same-namespace recreation is identified as a Stage-A physical-empty vestige that the incarnation makes unnecessary and should be deleted, not carried into the new removal design.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-fable-round2.md#2-3-the-answer-for-the-design-i-recommend-no-retirement-anywhere"]}
